enics the  idea is that you could in a  a wide range of occupations  take a second go do this for three four  minutes get back to your chair and not  be disruptive and not have to like drive  to the gym change clothes take a shower  etc  so this is an area of research that is  just getting rolling but  these are some nice strategies if you're  someone who was uh really  interested by greg's previous segments  about walking but you don't necessarily  do that well psychologically with uh  highly uh quantitative approaches to  exercise if you prefer to or if you just  don't have time to get in that many  steps like it is time consuming exactly  yeah but you know i the the  time-consuming component is big but i've  also seen instances in the physique  athlete world where people  will tend to take things to extremes you  know psychologically it is an extreme  endeavor and sometimes people respond  really unfavorably when you say hey try  to walk 15 000 steps per day and it's  like they are so fixated on that number  all day it's it's really uh detrimental  to quality of life for some people so  there's a lot of ways to break up your  sedentary activity and there's ample  evidence to suggest that it can be a  really positive thing to do so so i've  actually got  an anecdote related to exercise snacks  cool so back in the day uh  i think we've talked about this on the  podcast before i read a book  many of you have probably heard of  called the four hour body by tim ferriss  and  do not count this as a recommendation to  read that book there's  some good practical advice  mixed with some uh not so good advice  not the best signal-to-noise ratio  you'll come across uh but  one of the things he recommended uh  for  i think completely incorrect reasons did  has actually turned out to be relatively  helpful for me um  so he had this whole section about just  like hey how how can you use the magic  of nutrient repartitioning to  ensure that you can eat a bunch of food  and and 